      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 10000,
      "caption": "Loss-based schemes fill buffers; BBR aims to keep queues short.",
      "narration": "Loss-based algorithms keep pushing until a buffer overflows, so queues stay full. BBR targets the knee, with high throughput and a small queue.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Why BBR differs",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "l",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Reno/CUBIC: loss means congestion",
            "On lossy links they back off too much",
            "BBR does not treat loss as the signal"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "f",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "l",
          "at": 6000,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 9000,
      "caption": "On Linux you choose it per system or per socket.",
      "narration": "On Linux the algorithm is a sysctl, and ss shows live cwnd and rtt per connection.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Try it on Linux",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "c",
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "text",
          "text": "sysctl net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control\nsysctl -w \\\n net.ipv4.tcp_congestion_control=bbr\nss -ti",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "n",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "ss -ti prints cwnd, rtt, and the algorithm.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 56,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 4000,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "f",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "c",
          "at": 6000,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "takeaway": "Reno and CUBIC react to loss with different growth curves; BBR instead estimates bandwidth and RTT and paces to that model.",
  "sources": [
